<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lata Mangeshkar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/2022">2022</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hemalata Mangeshkar, Lata Deenanath Mangeshkar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lata Mangeshkar, born Hemalata in Indore, was an Indian playback singer whose career in film music spanned eight decades and who recorded songs in more than thirty-six Indian languages. The daughter of the classical singer and actor Deenanath Mangeshkar, she began working after his death in 1942 and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> in 1945; her song Aayega Aanewaala in the film Mahal (1949) helped win recognition for playback singers, until then uncredited. In 1963, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-indian-war">Sino-Indian War</a>, she sang Aye Mere Watan Ke Logo before Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>. She received the Dadasaheb Phalke Award in 1989 and the Bharat Ratna in 2001, and died in 2022 after treatment for COVID-19 and pneumonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lata-mangeshkar.jpg" alt="Lata Mangeshkar" width="330" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bollywood Hungama · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lata_Mangeshkar_-_still_29065_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Hussain Khan Bandanawazi, Farid Ayaz and Abu Muhammad Qawwal, Jafar Hussain Khan, the sitar and sarod players Ustad Vilayat Khan and Ustad Shujaat Khan, Zila Khan, the Nizami Bandhu, Hadiqa Kiani, Smita Bellur, <b>Lata Mangeshkar</b>, Asha Bhosle, Rahat Fateh Ali Khan, Kailash Kher, Kavita Seth and Maithili Thakur.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chhaap-tilak-sab-chheeni">Chhaap Tilak Sab Chheeni</a></li><li><blockquote>1, and a Hindi version, sung by <b>Lata Mangeshkar</b> and Mohammed Rafi, was used in the film Singapore.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rasa-sayang">Rasa Sayang</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lata Mangeshkar</b> later recorded it to a third, quite different tune.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li><li><blockquote>Hemant Kumar's setting for the film Anand Math, sung by <b>Lata Mangeshkar</b>, came second in a BBC World Service poll of the world's best-known songs in 2002, and the All India Radio version, often credited to Ravi Shankar, is in raga Desh.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Indore, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.72,75.86&amp;year=1989" aria-label="Indore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6579 1325 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lata Mangeshkar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7029 1595h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7029 1595h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.72,75.86&amp;year=1989">What was here in 1989? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
